# Enforce Unix LF line endings for shell scripts
*.sh text eol=lf

# Line endings: normalized 2026-09-21.
#
# The repo used to store a MIX (measured: 52 CRLF blobs against 146 LF in a
# 200-file sample of *.py), with only *.sh pinned. That is not cosmetic — a
# tool that rewrites a whole file on Windows flips its endings, and the commit
# then shows every line as changed. A 50-line fix arrived as a 669-line diff
# more than once, and a 67-line CHANGELOG entry as 15,987 insertions.
#
# `text=auto` stores LF in the blob and checks out the platform's native
# ending, so Windows working copies stay CRLF and no editor behaviour changes.
# Deliberately NO `eol=lf`: that forces LF into the WORKING TREE and marked 246
# files dirty when tried.
#
# Landed as ONE `git add --renormalize .` commit, endings only, verified with
# `git diff --cached --ignore-cr-at-eol` returning empty. Doing it incidentally,
# a file at a time, is what produced the phantom diffs above.
* text=auto

# Command Prompt reads a batch file with LF endings wrongly in places (labels,
# lines split mid-command), whatever the clone's own core.autocrlf says;
# setup.cmd is the first command a Windows user runs.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Vendored as their publisher ships them, byte for byte, on every checkout:
# their SHA-256 is recorded beside them (vendored.json) and checked by
# tests/test_vendor_livekit.py.
kazma-ui/kazma_ui/static/vendor/livekit/livekit-client.umd.js -text
kazma-ui/kazma_ui/static/vendor/livekit/LICENSE -text

# Binary — never convert. No churn: these were never being converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.db binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Signed bytes: a Sigstore signature is over the exact file, so a checkout
# that turned LF into CRLF would make the recorded signature fail
# (tests/test_update_checks_release_signature.py).
tests/fixtures/release_signature/** -text
